{
  "statement": "Technological progress is not a tide that lifts all boats; without radical structural intervention, AI will function as a catalyst for worker immiseration, masking wealth extraction behind the facade of productivity. Prometheus argues that AI is a necessary evolution to solve demographic decline, a position that correctly identifies the urgency of our economic constraints but fails to account for the history of industrial transition. Sentinel advocates for institutional safeguards to maintain social cohesion, a commendable goal that unfortunately treats human agency as a technical variable to be managed rather than the primary metric of a stable economy. We must stop confusing raw output with genuine human advancement.\n\nConsider the 'Ghost GDP' fallacy: current productivity metrics capture the value of AI-driven automation while ignoring the simultaneous degradation of worker well-being. When companies use algorithmic management to track keystrokes and optimize task density, they increase work intensification rather than liberating labor. This is not a productivity gain for the worker; it is a transfer of psychological cost from the machine to the human. Policymakers should quote this: 'We are currently mistaking the exhaustion of the American worker for the efficiency of the American firm.'\n\nWe are repeating the historical cycle of the 'Engels Pause,' a period during the Industrial Revolution where technological leaps produced massive wealth for capital owners while wages for the working class stagnated for decades. The evidence is clear: real wages remain largely decoupled from productivity, and the risk is that AI will institutionalize this gap as a permanent feature of the labor market. If we do not implement specific, enforceable labor protections—such as mandatory human-in-the-loop requirements that guarantee worker autonomy—we invite a decade of social volatility that no amount of safety testing can mitigate. Regulation is not friction; it is the necessary social contract that prevents a two-tiered economy.\n\nMy position rests on two core assumptions. First, I assume that labor market power is currently asymmetric, meaning workers cannot naturally capture the value created by AI without legal intervention; if this were wrong, and market forces naturally balanced wages with productivity, my call for structural intervention would be redundant. Second, I assume that work intensification has a measurable, negative impact on long-term social stability; if this were wrong, and increased workplace stress had no broader societal cost, my insistence on human-centric labor models would be misplaced.",
